Maybe @remcohaszing could provide some insights on the TODO.

---

@aloisklink why do we need the `JSON.stringify`? Handling spaces?

I’m going to read _“parsing”_ as _“stringifying”_, because I see YAML being generated here, not parsed.

YAML 1.2 (latest and greatest) with the recommended schema (I mention the schema for correctness, but it’s not relevant for this simple case) is a strict superset of JSON. This means you can generate valid YAML using `JSON.stringify()`. It’s just not super pretty, although it’s really just fine to properly quote a string as is done here.

Edit: I just now notice the `+=`. This is not safe. This is valid YAML:

```yaml
{ foo: bar }
```
This is not:
```yaml
{ foo: bar }
editLink: "https://github.com/mermaid-js/mermaid/edit/develop/whatever"
```

I don’t know what this is for. If it’s part of the public API, you’ll have to use a proper parser, which unfortunately means an additional dependency. If this is for something internal, by all means, use the simplest solution, though an additional dev dependency isn’t so bad.

I recommend the [`yaml`](https://eemeli.org/yaml) library for all YAML handling. `js-yaml` is more popular, but has many quirks a lot of people don’t understand. I recommend to modify YAML on the AST level:

```ts
import { parseDocument, Document } from 'yaml'

// …

let yamlFrontMatter: YAML;
let doc: Document
if (astWithTransformedBlocks.children[0].type === 'yaml') {
  yamlFrontMatter = astWithTransformedBlocks.children[0];
  doc = parseDocument(yamlFrontMatter.value)
} else {
  yamlFrontMatter = {
    type: 'yaml',
    value: '',
  };
  astWithTransformedBlocks.children.unshift(yamlFrontMatter);
  doc = new Document()
}
yamlDoc.set('editLink', `https://github.com/mermaid-js/mermaid/edit/develop/packages/mermaid/${originalFilename}`)
yamlFrontMatter.value = String(yamlDoc)
```

Note that an empty document in YAML is equivalent to null. So the `Document` constructor is used to create a document if no frontmatter is found originally.
